2. Overall Description

2.1 Product Perspective

AI-TRAVELMATE will be developed as a web-based application with a frontend, backend API, data integration layer, intelligence layer, and database.

The frontend will provide the user interface for entering preferences, viewing recommendations, exploring maps, and managing itineraries.

The backend will process requests, retrieve external data, apply recommendation and itinerary logic, and coordinate machine learning components.

The system will store relevant application records in a database where persistence is required.

2.2 Product Functions

The main system functions are:

1. Destination search and discovery.
2. Personalized destination recommendations.
3. Weather retrieval and weather-aware suggestions.
4. Route calculation and estimated travel duration.
5. Traffic prediction when suitable data is available.
6. AI-assisted travel explanations.
7. Itinerary generation and editing.
8. Saved trip management, if persistence is enabled.
9. Error handling and data-source transparency.

2.3 User Classes

General User

Can search destinations, provide preferences, explore recommendations, and create itineraries.

Registered User

If account functionality is implemented, can save and retrieve personal itineraries and preferences.

Administrator or Developer

Can configure application settings, manage data sources, inspect errors, and maintain model versions.

Administrative functionality is primarily intended for development and maintenance in the MVP.

2.4 Operating Environment

The application is intended to operate in:

- Modern desktop web browsers.
- Modern mobile web browsers.
- A frontend development environment supporting React and TypeScript.
- A Python environment supporting FastAPI and the selected ML libraries.
- A PostgreSQL database environment.

The application may use external internet services for maps, weather, routing, and points of interest.

A local AI model may require additional memory, storage, and processing capacity.

2.5 Design and Implementation Constraints

- The system should prioritize free or open-source technologies.
- External APIs may have usage limits and service availability constraints.
- OpenStreetMap data does not automatically include live traffic observations.
- Basic OSRM routing does not provide live congestion information.
- Traffic prediction quality depends on the availability and quality of representative training data.
- Local language model performance depends on the selected model and available hardware.
- The system must comply with relevant data licenses, API terms, attribution rules, and privacy requirements.

2.6 Assumptions and Dependencies

- The user has access to a compatible browser.
- Internet connectivity is available for external data retrieval.
- Selected services remain available and permit the intended usage.
- Geographical coordinates are available or can be resolved for supported destinations.
- Suitable traffic data may not be available for every location.
- The availability of local traffic data must be verified before local traffic prediction is treated as a supported feature.

